// @error an object of type OW cannot flow into ON: they agree on field names but differ INSIDE a field, and a struct field is mutable — writing through the narrower view would leave the original holding a value of the wrong type, so the two are unrelated by design and not by a missing lowering. Give the source exactly ON's field types, or read the field out and rebuild
// D938 — THE NESTED WIDTH PAIR, and this file pins that it is a DESIGN refusal rather than a
// capability one. It used to be check-clean INVALID WASM: `objShapeAdapterless` returned
// adapterless as soon as `objFieldNameSetsEq` answered, so the checker accepted a pair the
// engine then refused — a validator catching a type-system hole by accident, and a clause-1
// soundness violation with no corpus cell.
//
// Unlike the DIRECT width flow (D622, which now runs and prints 1 — the WasmGC `(sub)` edge is
// free and keeps reference identity), this one cannot be closed by making it run. A struct's
// mutable field is INVARIANT in WasmGC, so no subtype edge between `{n: Wide}` and
// `{n: Narrow}` exists to declare; a write through the narrower view would leave the original
// holding a value of the wrong type. There is no sound lowering to build, only a refusal owed.
//
// THE MESSAGE MATTERS AS MUCH AS THE REFUSAL. Every neighbouring wording in
// `objShapeAdapterlessErr` concedes the program is type-valid and blames codegen — the
// clause-2 sentence `goal-scoreboard.py` counts. This one goes on the plain `tErr` channel and
// says what is actually wrong, so closing a soundness hole did not open a capability one.
//
// The ACCEPTING side of the same axis is `object-shape-unannotated-literal-rebuilt.vl`: an
// UN-ANNOTATED source binding has no pinned wasm type, so the emitter lays it out at the shape
// it flows into and there is no second view to write through. That one still runs.
type Wide = { a: i32, b: i32 }
type Narrow = { a: i32 }
type OW = { n: Wide }
type ON = { n: Narrow }

function take(o: ON): i32 {
  (o.n).a
}

const w: OW = { n: { a: 1, b: 2 } }

print(take(w))
